  "checklist": [
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Supply VDD33 within 3.0 to 3.6 V (3.3 V typical) and able to deliver at least 0.5 A.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 22,
        "where": "Recommended Operating Conditions"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Fit 10 uF and 0.1 uF on the 3V3 pin as in the reference schematic.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 34,
        "where": "Figure 9-1"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "recommended",
      "text": "Place C1 and C2 as close to the module's 3V3 pin as practical, with a short return to the ground pad.",
      "basis": "convention"
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "Never leave EN floating. Add the RC delay (usually 10 kOhm and 1 uF) so the supply is stable before the chip starts, and check it against your power-up timing: the rails need at least 50 us to stabilise before EN goes high, and EN must stay low for at least 50 us to reset.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 34,
        "where": "EN pin p. 11; note under Figure 9-1; timing Table 4-6, p. 15"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "GPIO2, GPIO8 and GPIO9 are strapping pins, read at reset. For normal boot from flash GPIO2 and GPIO9 must be high (GPIO8 can be anything); for download boot GPIO2 and GPIO8 must be high and GPIO9 low. GPIO2 and GPIO8 float by default and GPIO9 has a weak pull-up, so the reference circuit pulls GPIO2 and GPIO8 up with 10 kOhm resistors (R9, R8). The datasheet recommends the GPIO2 pull-up because of glitches.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 13,
        "where": "Tables 4-1 and 4-3; Figure 9-1"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "To start the bootloader, hold GPIO9 low while EN is released: bridge JP2, then reset. Both UART0 and the USB port work as the download interface in this mode. The strapping pins must hold their level for at least 3 ms after EN goes high, so do not drive them from anything else at that moment.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 13,
        "where": "Table 4-2 and Table 4-3, Joint Download Boot"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "USB uses IO18 (D-, pin 13) and IO19 (D+, pin 14). The reference keeps 0 ohm series resistors R6 and R4 on both lines; C5 and C6 are placeholders with no value and not fitted.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 34,
        "where": "Figure 9-1; pin table p. 11"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "info",
      "text": "UART0 (RXD = GPIO20 on pin 11, TXD = GPIO21 on pin 12) is on JP4 for firmware download and log output. JTAG is on IO4 (MTMS), IO5 (MTDI), IO6 (MTCK) and IO7 (MTDO), on JP1.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 34,
        "where": "Figure 9-1; pin table p. 11"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "recommended",
      "text": "Soldering the exposed ground pad (EPAD) is optional but improves thermal performance. Use the right amount of solder paste: too much lifts the module and spoils the other joints.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 34,
        "where": "note under Figure 9-1"
      }
    },
    {
      "severity": "required",
      "text": "The ESP32-C3-WROOM-02 has an on-board antenna, with a keep-out zone marked on the pin diagram (the -02U has none, for an external antenna). Keep copper, ground pour and components out of the keep-out zone and place the module as the Hardware Design Guidelines describe. Check the footprint against the recommended land pattern.",
      "basis": "datasheet",
      "source": {
        "doc": "ds",
        "page": 10,
        "where": "Note A; Sec. 11.2 p. 39; land pattern p. 38"
      }
    }
  ],
